<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Land of Suhu</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 616 BCE (approx.) · capital Anat</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A semi-independent principality on the middle Euphrates whose governors claimed ancient descent and boasted of introducing honeybees to their land.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Suhu stretched along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a>, with its centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anah">Anat</a> (modern Ana), an island town in the river. It was a recognised province of Babylonia by the 9th century BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurnasirpal-ii">Ashurnasirpal II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> campaigned against it in 878 BCE when it sheltered his enemies.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Suhu's governors ruled as hereditary lords, nominally loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> but often acting independently on the caravan route along the river. In the 8th century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamash-resha-usur">Shamash-resha-usur</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninurta-kudurri-usur-of-suhu">Ninurta-kudurri-usur</a> left long Akkadian inscriptions describing canal building, the defeat of Aramean raiders and, in Shamash-resha-usur's case, bringing honeybees from the mountains of the Habha people and learning to make honey and wax. Its population mixed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaeans">Arameans</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Suhu later came under Assyrian control. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-chronicles">Babylonian Chronicle</a> records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabopolassar">Nabopolassar</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a> took Suhu in 616 BCE as he advanced against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, ending its separate identity.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 900 BCE – 616 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:41.79%;width:5.65%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 616 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Anat</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Shamash-resha-usur, Ninurta-kudurri-usur</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mesopotamian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Akkadian, Aramaic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Suhu, Suhum</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-758&amp;focus=land-of-suhu" aria-label="Land of Suhu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5771 966 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Land of Suhu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6092 1134h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6091 1222h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6091 1222h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iraq. <a href="/atlas?year=-758&amp;focus=land-of-suhu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

